## 1. INTRODUCTION
Detecting methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ at leak relevant levels under normal atmospheric conditions is crucial to industrial safety and environmental protection. First, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is highly flammable, exhibiting a deflagration to detonation transition in the range of $5\mathrm{-}15\%$ by volume [i.e., $\sim50,000{-}150,000$ parts per million $\left(\mathrm{{ppm}}\right)]$ . Undesired release of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ during coal extractions, leaks from natural gas pipelines, or outbursts can contribute to catastrophic disasters if not identified and mitigated quickly.1 Furthermore, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is known to be 21 times more potent as a greenhouse gas than carbon dioxide $(\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ with an atmospheric lifetime of roughly a decade due to its very low reactivity to lose or gain an electron under atmospheric conditions.2 Based on Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change’s research, at least a quarter of today’s global warming is driven by $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ from anthropogenic sources, and it is a key precursor to tropospheric ozone generation that is harmful for our ecosystem as an air pollutant. Then, there is an urgent need to detect fugitive $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at a lower concentration than the global ambient concentration level of $1.8~\pm~0.2$ ppm.2,3 However, even with the current state-of-the-art detection techniques that include infrared spectroscopy,4 modulation spectroscopy,5 Raman spectroscopy,6 gas chromatography,7 photothermal spectroscopy,8 and pyroelectric sensors,9 lowcost and high-performance detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at very low concentrations with a miniaturized integrated gas sensor is still a formidable challenge. Therefore, an unmet need still exists for developing novel, cost-effective, and reliable miniaturized sensors with high sensitivity and selectivity toward ppb concentration levels of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
In recent years, the advances in micro-/nano-fabrication technology and the use of newly designed nanomaterials have facilitated the development of cost-effective miniaturized integrated gas sensors that have enhanced sensitivity and selectivity toward various target gases.10 Among newly designed materials, metal oxides, graphene-based composites, carbon nanotubes (CNTs), and polymers have been rigorously investigated as gas sensing materials.1 However, the generally weak interaction of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with these materials led to very short residual time in the order of $10^{-10}$ seconds making detection at very low concentrations a considerable challenge.1,8,11 To enhance sorption and residual time, nanoporous materials like metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have been proposed. MOFs self-assemble via coordination bonds of metal nodes with organic ligands, presenting a novel class of thermally stable and chemically inert crystalline hybrid nanoporous material with active sites of varying electron affinity. Their permanent porosity, tunable pore size, and functionalizable internal pore surfaces are conducive to rendering enhanced chemical sensitivity and selectivity that has been explored at length.12−14 As such, MOFs are being routinely investigated for detection of polar molecules, organic molecules, and ions.15−18 However, only a few MOF-based $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sensors have been reported so far, and the improvement in selectivity and sensitivity is still required before MOFs could be widely used as CH4 sensors.19−23
MOFs in general are excellent for high-pressure $(\sim35$ bar) gas storage applications owing to their high surface area and free volume allowing high gas uptake. However, at atmospheric pressures, these factors do not correlate, and their adsorption capacity is low.24 This severely limits real-time gas sensing applications with MOFs and results in low sensitivity, especially for low reactive gas like $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ which generally has weak interaction.1 Moreover, the presence of ambient moisture in the order of a few parts per thousand under normal atmospheric conditions often leads to false positives, making selective detection a formidable challenge in most sensing materials, specifically in MOFs.25,26 Another key limitation of most MOFs as sensing materials is their intrinsically low electrical conductivity (lower than $10^{-10}\ {\mathrm{Scm}}^{-1}.$ ).27 Based on experimental and theoretical studies, the basic reasons for this poor conductivity are the lack of charge carrier mobility and limited energy transport pathways because electron transfer proceeds weakly between non-redox-active metal centers and organic linkers.28,29 This severely hinders their applicability in making electrical sensors. Most reports in this area therefore focus on optical sensors30−32 or more complicated gravimetric sensors such as MOF-coated microcantilevers33 and quartz crystal microbalances.34−36 However, if MOFs can be tailored to improve electrical transport properties, adsorption/ desorption kinetics can be monitored electrically by exploiting the property of varying electron affinity at their different adsorption sites. Thus, overcoming this electrical conductivity issue has been attempted by various routes such as compositing them with conducting/semiconducting nanomaterials [e.g., quantum dots, nanoparticles (NPs), 2D-graphene, and CNTs], by post-synthetic modification, or via incorporation of guest molecules. $^{27,28,37-39}$ In this work, we use bare MWCNTs to make a composite sensing material.
In the last 2 decades, CNT-based sensors have demonstrated huge potential and found applications in gas sensing because of their unique morphology and electrical properties. However, pristine (bare) CNTs are chemically inert, exhibiting strong $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ bonds and weak $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ bonds at the tube ends, making them impossible to be utilized for gas sensing (especially for nonpolar gases with low reactivity such as $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ to accept or donate electrons).40−42 Thus, CNTs have been typically modified with functional groups, dopants, metallic NPs, metal-oxide $\mathrm{NPs}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ and conductive polymers to overcome this limitation.1,43,44 We tmakoediafincatlitoern otif MroWutCeNfoTlls winitgh cMenOtFrse.4p5,o4r6t Ioncocropmorpaotsiintge MWCNTs in MOF architecture as a composite not only can combine the properties of both MWCNTs and MOFs for obtaining superior sensing performance but also could address the limitation of both MOFs and MWCNTs for detection of low reactive and non-polar gases like $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ Although there are a few reports on use of the MOF/MWCNT composite for detection of volatile organic compounds $(\mathrm{VOCs}),^{45^{\circ},46}$ to our knowledge, there is no study on use of this composite for detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . In most of the literature, functionalized MWCNTs were used to make MOF/MWCNT composites.47,48 However, as a result of functionalization, MWCNTs could undergo important changes and lose their original properties, such as losing their hydrophobicity which is desirable for gas sensing applications. Therefore, using bare MWCNTs, which are super-hydrophobic, could be a promising way to improve the overall hydrophobicity of their composite with MOFs.
In this work, we introduce a nanocomposite of an MOF with bare MWCNTs, which has a unique electrical property for selective detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at room temperature (RT). Within the class of $\mathrm{MOFs}$ , we chose ZIF-8 for its low water affinity and good stability under ambient conditions. This is the first ever usage of the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite as a sensing layer of a chemiresistive sensor for selective detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . By making this nanocomposite, we demonstrate enhancement of electrical conductivity and hydrophobicity. Furthermore, we exploit its unique electrical transport enhancement for detecting $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at a very low concentration of $10\ \mathrm{ppb}$ under normal atmospheric conditions with a determined limit of detection (LOD) of $\sim0.22~\mathrm{ppb}$ , hitherto unprecedented. Our composite approach is guided by the possibility of decreasing the electronic band gap and activation energy of ZIF-8 by a random incorporation of MWCNTs. For further evaluations of our sensor performance, responsivity, reversibility, selectivity, response and recovery time, sensitivity, repeatability, and linearity are systematically investigated.
## 2. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
2.1. Structural and Morphological Characterization of the ZIF-8, MWCNTs, and ZIF-8/MWCNT Nanocomposite. In this work, in situ growth of ZIF-8 in the presence of bare MWCNTs is achieved through an in situ sonochemical synthesis method to make the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite. Figure 1a illustrates a schematic drawing of the preparation process of the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite and its spray-coated thin film on the surface of an interdigitated electrode (IDE) chip. A Zeta optical profilometer was used for the ZIF-8/MWCNT film thickness measurements and defect inspection (Figures 1b and S1a). The obtained nanocomposite film has great uniformity with a thickness of ${\sim}8\mu\mathrm{m}$ without any defect. The morphology of the synthesized ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite and its elements are investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM)/ energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) analysis (Figures $_{\mathrm{1c-e}}$ and S2). SEM images show ZIF-8’s narrow size distribution with particle sizes ${\sim}50~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figures 1e and S1b) and the existence of MWCNT strands among ZIF-8 crystals (Figure 1e). These strands link the crystals to each other and make paths for electron transfer, which gives a semiconductive property to the nanocomposite. Alternatively, we can describe that the MWCNTs could act as nucleation sites for the crystallization of ZIF-8 forming a dense beaded network.
Information) (Figure $^{2\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c})}$ . As reported so far, ZIF-8 is considered an insulator with a wide band gap of around 5.5 $\mathrm{eV.}^{27,51}$ However, the synthesized ZIF-8 nanoparticle $(\sim50$ nm) here has intrinsic semiconducting properties with a band gap of $3.2\ \mathrm{{\eV}}$ . This reduction in the band gap could be attributed to exceptional electrical properties of nanoparticles under size reduction.52,53 As in intrinsic semiconductors, no free charge carriers are available for conduction (the number of electrons and holes is equal) resulting in large activation or operation energy $(\Delta E);^{5\hat{4}}$ synthesized ZIF-8 does not show any electrical conduction at RT. However, by making a composite of synthesized ZIF-8 with bare MWCNTs, a p-type semiconductor behavior with a narrower band gap of $2.87~\mathrm{eV}$ has been obtained. From the energy gap viewpoint, existence of MWCNTs in the nanocomposite creates energy levels within the band gap very close to the valence band, which are called acceptor levels $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ . This shifts the effective Fermi level to a point about halfway between the acceptor levels and the valence band to show a p-type semiconductor behavior.54 In ptype semiconductors, electrons can be easily excited from the valence band into acceptor levels, leaving mobile holes in the valence band that can contribute to good electrical conduction at RT, and small activation energy could significantly enhance gas-sensing performance due to the easier sharing of electrons to make van der Waals interactions with target gas.
For further characterization, Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy, Raman spectroscopy, and $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) analysis for the bare MWCNTs, ZIF-8, and ZIF-8/ MWCNT nanocomposite have been performed (see Figure 3 and the Supporting Information for detailed analysis). The inherent hydrophobicity of ZIF-8 is evident from its FT-IR spectrum band at $3455~\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ (attributed to the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretching vibration of residual Hmim on the outer surface of ZIF-8 and the $_\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching vibration of physically adsorbed water) which is weak in comparison to that of other kinds of MOFs.45 The successful incorporation of MWCNTs into the ZIF-8 structure in making the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite without having any chemical interactions is evident in its comparable FT-IR and Raman peaks with those of ZIF-8 without any new bands (Figure 3a,b).55 Comparable peaks in their XRD patterns (Figure 3c) also suggest that the ZIF-8’s crystalline structure is maintained even after the incorporation of MWCNTs. However, the intensity of peaks for ZIF-8/ MWCNTs has reduced in the FT-IR spectrum and increased in the XRD pattern, suggesting the existence of MWCNTs in the nanocomposite and an enhancement in the crystallinity in the nanocomposite, respectively. Moreover, in the Raman spectrum of ZIF-8/MWCNTs, the G band is shifted compared to that in the pure MWCNTs, suggesting the formation of the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite. Typically, the $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio in the Raman spectrum represents defect density in MWCNT structures; in our case, we obtained 0.87 for bare MWCNTs and 0.92 for ZIF-8/MWCNTs (Figure 3b). These obtained values of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ suggest the presence of defect sites in our bare MWCNTs. Such defects are expected to induce metallic to semiconducting transitions resulting in complex conductance variations.
2.2. Sensor Performance Evaluation. To evaluate gassensing performance, the spray-coated IDE chips with the ZIF8/MWCNT nanocomposite (5 wt $\%$ of MWCNTs) are exposed to predetermined concentrations of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ CO, $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ toluene, benzene, methanol, ethanol, isopropanol, and water vapor. Physically, upon exposure to different gas/ vapor analytes, molecules are physisorbed into the pores and surfaces of ZIF-8 via accepting an electron from or donating an electron to the ZIF-8/MWCNT layer (Figure 4a). The net exchanged electrons from the sensing layer to gas/vapor analytes can be negative $\left(-\Delta\mathrm{e}^{-}\right)$ , positive $(+\Delta{\mathrm e}^{-})$ , or zero $\left(\Delta\mathrm{e}^{-}=0\right)$ resulting in decrease, increase, or no changes in normalized resistance change $\left(\Delta R/R_{0}\right)$ , respectively.
For investigating our sensor’s $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ responsivity, the coated IDE chips with ZIF-8/5 wt $\%$ MWCNTs were initially exposed to 200 standard cubic centimeter per minute $(\mathsf{s c c m})$ dry air to record the baseline resistance $\left(R_{0}\right)$ , followed by exposing to desired $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ concentrations $\left(1.5\mathrm{-}100\ \mathrm{ppm}\right)$ at a total flow rate of 200 sccm until sensor response reached a steady state (Figure S3). Subsequently, a dry-air flow cycle was run to recover the baseline confirming reversibility (Figure 4b) which can be attributed to physisorption without chemical bonding. The considerable responsivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ down to $1.5\ \mathrm{ppm}$ has roots in the significantly smaller band gap of our composite and smaller activation energy $(\Delta E)$ (Figure 2c), allowing easier sharing of electrons between the composite and the gas/vapor molecules to make van der Waals interactions causing higher gas uptake in our system. In contrast to $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ $100\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ results in a slight increase in normalized resistance change $\left(+\Delta{\sf e^{-}}\right)$ (Figure $\mathrm{4c}\mathrm{\"{\$ ). However, in the case of ethanol, methanol, and isopropanol vapors, the sensor response is negligible ( $\left(\Delta\mathrm{e}^{-}=0\right)$ (Figure $\scriptstyle4c$ ). Moreover, to evaluate our sensor’s selectivity toward $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the presences of these vapors, our sensor was exposed to binary mixtures of $100\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with each of these vapors, resulting in nearly identical response compared to $100\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ alone (Figure 4d) with appreciable reversibility, attesting to selective sensing performance in mixtures. This selectivity can be attributed to ZIF-8’s different adsorption sites with varying electron affinity which is explained in detail in Section 2.3. The sensor responses to CO, ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ toluene, and benzene were also investigated for additional evaluation of our sensor selectivity (Figure S4a,b, Supporting Information for details). Our sensor showed high selectivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the presence of these gases/vapors.
One key challenge of various sensing layers explored in the literature to date for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is the potential false positives with ambient moisture.1 Thus, from the perspective of maintaining reliability, it is very important to eliminate or mitigate the dependence of sensor response on humidity. Generally, the concentration of moisture in the atmosphere ranges from 6000 to 12,000 ppm $30-80\%$ relative humidity (RH)].56 To investigate the response of our sensor to ambient moisture, it was exposed to different humidity levels. Contrary to $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ water vapor results in a slight increase in normalized resistance change $\left(+\Delta{\sf e^{-}}\right)$ (Figure 4e). Nevertheless, the positive change in resistance to $13,662\mathrm{ppm}$ of water vapor, which is more than $80\%$ RH, remains significantly less than that in the response to $1.5~\mathrm{\ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . This can be attributed to the inherent hydrophobicity of ZIF-8 at the inner network of its pores with the limited adsorption site on its outer surface which we will discuss further in Section 2.3.57 To investigate the performance of our sensor for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ detection in a humid environment, it was exposed to mixtures of $100\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with $24{,}327\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of humidity (more than $90\%$ RH). The results show that the sensitivity of our sensor to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is not affected by the presence of humidity even at $\mathrm{RH}>90\%$ (Figure 4d), confirming that $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules do not compete for the same adsorption sites in the ZIF-8 pores which will be discussed in detail in the Response Mechanism section.
For more evaluation of our sensor, stability, the effect of different total flow rates on repeatability, linearity, sensitivity (Figures $S4c{-}S7'$ ), and variation of sensing layer thickness on normalized resistance change were also investigated (Figure S8). Furthermore, to investigate the variation of the MWCNT concentration in the composite’s effect on our sensor performance, newly coated IDE chips with ZIF-8/10 wt $9\%$ MWCNTs were exposed to repeating cycles of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (10 ppb to $100\ \mathrm{ppm})$ and dry-air flow as before (Figure 4f). The relative resistance change as a function of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ concentration is normalized and fitted as a power-law dependence, shown in Figure $^{4}\mathrm{g},$ with the $x$ -intercept representing the LOD $\sim0.22$ ppb. The normalized minimum measurable limit was determined to be in the order of 0.17 corresponding to the $3\times$ noise floor at 200 sccm flow of dry air. Generally, gas adsorption in porous materials occurs by three steps of the adsorption, diffusion, and desorption process.58 This nonlinear response of our system to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ could be attributed to the dominancy of the surface adsorption effect at lower concentrations and the bulk diffusion effect at higher concentrations. Compared to the ZIF-8/5 wt $\%$ MWCNTbased sensor for detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ the ZIF-8/10 wt $\%$ MWCNT-based sensor shows a slight increase in normalized resistance change which can be attributed to its higher uptake of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at atmospheric pressure due to increase in electron transport and the easier sharing of electrons to make van der Waals interactions with $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Figure 4h). Interestingly, the response and recovery times are significantly reduced with the increase in the MWCNT concentration because more MWCNTs facilitate a faster rate of charge transfer on adsorption (Figure 4i).
2.3. Response Mechanism Based on Structural and Electrical Properties of ZIF-8 and MWCNTs. It is imperative to evaluate each role of MWCNTs and ZIF-8 in the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite in the sensing mechanism that instigates significant responsivity of the sensor to the ppb concentration level of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at atmospheric pressure with ultrahigh selectivity. This significant responsivity can be attributed to the role of conductive MWCNTs creating an acceptor level $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ within the band gap very close to the valence band. The ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite with a narrower band gap and smaller activation energy $(\Delta E)$ (Figure $\mathsf{2b},\mathsf{c}$ ) facilitates easier sharing of electrons between the composite and the gas molecules. The ultrahigh selectivity of our sensor corresponds to the role of ZIF-8 as a porous material with ultra-high surface area providing different adsorption sites with varying electron affinity for adsorption of gas molecules. To better represent our sensor’s selectivity mechanism, knowledge of these adsorption sites in ZIF-8 is crucial, which can be achieved by knowing the ZIF-8’s crystal and chemical structure. ZIF-8 crystals are formed by the attack of unconjugated lone pairs of neutral Hmim linkers on the partially positive terminal zinc atoms to form cages [called “sodalite (SOD) cages”] with eight hexagonal windows and six square windows. These cages are packed together to form the SOD framework (Figure 5a).59 The sharpest XRD peak at $7.74^{\circ}$ confirms that ZIF-8 crystals have a SOD morphology, having 12 exposed (110) faces (Figure 3c).46 Compared with many other MOFs, as ZIF-8 has no metal oxide cluster, its major adsorption sites are near imidazole rings instead of the metal oxide cluster, as shown in Figure 5a.60,61 Moreover, owing to the absence of hydrogen bonding groups at the inner network of its pores and uncoordinated centers from the inorganic ligands, adsorption of water molecules inside its pores is energetically unfavorable.57,62 However, the existence of $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ functionality of residual Hmim on the outer surface of ZIF-8 which are the only adsorption sites for making hydrogen bonding with water is responsible for limited adsorption of water vapor in our system (Figure 5b).63 Because of the electron-donating nature of water molecules and increase in the resistance of our sensor on exposure to humidity (Figure 4e), it can be concluded that the obtained ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite has a p-type semiconducting behavior. In other words, in a p-type semiconductor, the charge carriers are holes, and the adsorbed water molecules neutralize the hole carriers in our system by donating the electron, resulting in an increase in the resistance.
The exact adsorption sites for each of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and alcohols are shown in Figure 5b. Based on the literature,60 $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is mostly populated at site 1 through a sigma− $\cdot\pi$ electron interaction between $\pi$ electrons on the Hmim ring and sigma $\mathrm{C-H}$ bonds of $\mathrm{CH}_{4.}$ Thus, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ acts as an electron acceptor which results in the increase in the number of charge carrier (holes) in our p-type semiconducting layer and decreases the resistance in our system (Figure 4b). As for $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ the carbon atom is electron-deficient, and oxygen is electron-rich; it can act either as an electron acceptor or donor depending on the environment.64 In ZIF-8, as reported by Liu et al.,61 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules behave as an electron acceptor at sites 1 and 2 via the quadrupole- $\pi$ electron interaction between the $\pi$ electrons on the imidazole ring and electron-deficient $\mathrm{C}^{\delta+}\ (\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ and the weak electrostatic interaction between $\mathrm{C}^{\delta-}$ (methyl) and $\mathbf{C}^{\delta+}\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\right)$ , respectively. However, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can also behave as an electron donor at sites 3 and 4 through a hydrogen bond-like interaction between $\mathrm{O}^{\delta-}\ (\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ and $\check{\mathrm{H}}^{\delta+}$ (methyl) and a very weak electrostatic interaction between $\dot{\mathrm{~O~}^{\delta-}}$ $(\dot{\mathrm{CO}}_{2})$ and the positive electrostatic potential of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{\delta+}$ $\left(\mathrm{Hmim}\right)$ , respectively. The slight increase in our sensor’s resistance on exposure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 4c) indicates that the possibility of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ donating an electron is somewhat higher than that of it accepting an electron in our system, which is in agreement with experimental and molecular simulation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on ZIF-8.60
Based on the literature, alcohol sorption amounts in ZIFs are extremely low, and these low uptakes are disadvantageous for ZIF materials in alcohol extraction.65 Moreover, the ZIF-8 structure can be readily accessible to alcohol molecules through sites 1 and 4 in six-ring windows (Figure 5a).63 They act as an electron acceptor at site 1 through a sigma−π electron interaction between $\pi$ electrons on the Hmim ring and sigma $\mathrm{C-H}$ bonds of alcohol molecules and as an electron donor at site 4 via a very weak electrostatic interaction between $\mathrm{O}^{\delta-}$ (alcohols) and the positive electrostatic potential of $\mathrm{H}^{\delta+}$ $\left({\mathrm{Hmim}}\right)$ (Figure 5b). However, our sensor shows negligible responses to methanol, ethanol, and isopropanol (Figure 4c), which can be attributed to the fact that in competition between donating an electron and accepting an electron, there is no winner, and overall, we have negligible changes in charge density of our sensing layer.
## 3. CONCLUSION
We demonstrate the rapid, facile, and low-cost preparation of the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite and its first ever usage as a sensing layer of a chemiresistive sensor for the selective detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ under ambient conditions. The obtained ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite with a high surface area, narrow band gap, rapid carrier mobility, high stability, and nontoxic peculiarity has outstanding sensing performance in comparison to other reported materials for detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Table 1). Our composite approach renders unique p-type semiconducting behavior to the sensing layer making possible the detection of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at a very low concentration of $10\ \mathrm{ppb}$ with the LOD $\sim~0.22~\mathrm{\ppb}$ . Unprecedented sensitivity, appreciable repeatability, stability, reversibility, and high selectivity in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ CO, ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ alcohols, benzene, and toluene at RT and under ambient conditions are successfully demonstrated. Moreover, the low response of our sensor to humidity makes it an excellent candidate for realworld applications. We explain the mechanism of selective response to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ over $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ alcohols, and moisture based on different adsorption sites in ZIF-8 with varying electron affinity for adsorption of molecules. Beyond $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ selectivity to other non-polar gases with low reactivity could be engineered for other real-time demanding applications. Similar carbonengineered MOF nanocomposites will have the promise of a new class of sensing materials that have selective response to low reactive gases via carrier manipulation in the energy band gap.
## 4. MATERIALS AND METHODS
4.1. Synthesize of ZIF-8 and ZIF-8/MWCNT Nanoparticles To synthesize pure ZIF-8 nanoparticles, $83~\mathrm{mg}$ of Hmim $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{6}N_{2}},$ Sigma-Aldrich, $299\%$ ) and $93~{\mathrm{mg}}$ of zinc acetate dihydrate $(98\%$ , VWR) were separately dissolved in 16 and $5~\mathrm{\mL}$ of $^{N,N}$ - dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ , VWR), respectively. The precursor solutions were mixed, and the vial was set to the probe of an ultra-sonic generator (100 W, QSonica Q500, USA) at RT (around $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{\cdot}$ ) for $7~\mathrm{min}$ till we got a monodispersed white solution. Subsequently, ZIF-8 is separated from the reaction mixture by centrifugation $6000~\mathrm{rpm}$ , Corning C6755) and washed with ethanol $(30~\mathrm{mL})$ . The ZIF-8/MWCNT composite was prepared under the same conditions with the addition of $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of bare MWCNTs $\left(30-\right.$ $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ outer diameter, $10-20\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ long, and purity $>95$ wt $\%$ , Cheap Tubes) in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF into the precursor solutions. Instead of following the reported studies in using functionalized MWCNTs for making the MOF/MWCNT nanocomposite, for the first time, bare MWCNTs have been used in making the ZIF-8/MWCNT nanocomposite to engineer our nanocomposite selectivity and render an enhancement in overall hydrophobicity.
4.2. Material Characterization. ZIF-8, MWCNTs, and ZIF-8/ MWCNT were characterized by FT-IR (Nicolet Nexus 470, USA) to measure the transmittance spectra, XRD (Rigaku Multiflex) to characterize the crystalline structure, and Raman spectroscopy (Horiba XploRA PLUS, diode-pumped solid-state laser $\left(532\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ , power $14~\mathrm{mW}.$ , USA) to measure the Raman spectra for confirming their molecular structures. A field-emission scanning electron microscope (Quanta 250) was used to determine and analyze the morphology, and a UV−vis spectrophotometer (Ocean Optics, Flame-S, USA) was utilized to measure the light absorption properties to determine the optical band gap. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Hitachi H7650) was employed to characterize bare MWCNTs (see Figure S9 and the Supporting Information for detailed analysis). 4.3. Fabrication of a ZIF-8/MWCNT Thin Layer on the IDE Chip. For implementing spray-coating, stable colloidal suspensions of the synthesized ZIF-8/MWCNT were obtained by re-dispersing the washed product in ethanol. An airbrush double action kit (Pinkiou SP180KTG, USA) with a nozzle diameter of $0.2~\mathrm{\mm}$ and a compressor air tank was used to make a sensing layer of ZIF-8/ MWCNT on the surface of an IDE chip [overall size: $\mathsf{S}\mathrm{\mm}\times6\mathrm{\mm}$ $\times0.38~\mathrm{mm}$ , consisted of 18 gold fingers ( $90\mu\mathrm m$ wide, $3800\mu\mathrm{m}$ long. The gap between fingers is $90~\Bar{\mu}\mathrm{m})$ ] (Figure S10). The initial resistance of the coated IDE chips is about $100\Omega$ . The spraying rate was optimized to $3~\mathrm{mL/min}$ to obtain a uniform film. The distance between the tip of the nozzle and the substrate was optimized to 20 cm. The deposited time was varied to control the thickness of the sensing layer. The thickness of the deposited layer was measured with an optical profilometer (Zeta-20) and displayed a uniform thickness of $8\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ on $10\ \mathrm{min}$ spraying (Figures 1b and S1a).
4.4. Sensor Test Setup and Sensing Experiment. The gassensing properties were evaluated by using an in-house-fabricated flow chamber (Figure S11). Dry air was used as the carrier gas to verify the performance of the fabricated sensor in real-world applications. The mass flow controller (MFC, AtoVac GMC1200, Suwon, South Korea) modulates the gas flow rate at each line to control the gas concentration (Figure S12). The real-time resistance of the sensors was measured using a digital multimeter (Keysight 34461A, Malaysia). The measured resistance was converted into the normalized response value $\left[\Delta R/R_{0},\right.$ where $\Delta R=\left(R_{t}-R_{0}\right)$ is timedependent resistance change, where $R_{0}$ is the baseline resistance in dry air and $R_{t}$ is the measured resistance under gas/vapor exposure]. Before the measurement, the sensors were stabilized in the baseline dry air for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a total flow rate of $200\ \mathrm{sccm}$ . All the sensing tests were conducted at ambient pressure and RT. To control the humidity level, a bubbler filled with double distilled water was utilized, and an environmental sensor (Thorlabs-TSP01, USA) was used to monitor temperature and RH.
